  "turns": [
    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello, I'm glad you could make it in today. What brings you to the clinic?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S014",
      "patient_response": "Hi, yeah, thanks for seeing me. I appreciate it. It's, well, it's a bit of a long story, but I guess the main thing that brings me in today is that I just feel like I’m always moving from one thing to the next. I really just can’t seem to idle, you know? Like a car engine that's just constantly revving in neutral. \n\nI work as a customer service rep, and I've been doing that kind of work for a long time. Usually, I manage okay because I find ways to keep busy, but we recently had this whole overhaul at the office. They completely changed our floor plan, put us in these open pods where everyone can see everyone else, and they implemented this new strict scheduling system where you're glued to your headset. And, well, this new work setup has made my constant need to be doing something a lot harder to hide. \n\nIt used to be that if the phones were quiet, I could get up, organize some files, pace around the breakroom, just kind of burn off that nervous energy. But now, I'm supposed to just sit there and wait for the next call, and I physically can't do it. I'm shifting in my chair, tapping my feet, bouncing from one browser tab to another, getting up when I'm not supposed to... My supervisor actually pulled me aside last week because it's becoming so obvious. I just can't sit still and do nothing, and it's really starting to put my job on the line.",
