    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Looking back, did you experience this same kind of restlessness or trouble sitting still when you were a child?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, yeah, actually. It's funny you ask that because, honestly, looking back, this whole... whatever you want to call it, this restlessness, it was around long before this recent job change. I mean, way back. If I really think about it, I can picture being in grade school, and it was always the same story. My mom kept a box of all my old report cards in the attic, and I remember looking through them a few years ago when we were clearing out her house. Every single year, it seemed like the teachers wrote the exact same thing. They'd always say I was a bright kid, you know, that I had potential and all that, but then there was always a \"but.\" It was always, \"He's bright, but he's careless,\" or \"He rushes through things.\" \n\nAnd the talking—oh boy, the talking. They were always writing notes home about how I talked out of turn, how I couldn't just raise my hand and wait to be called on. It was like the thought would pop into my head and it just had to come out right that second, regardless of who else was talking. And staying at my desk? Forget about it. They constantly had to remind me to stay with my work, to sit down, to stop wandering over to the pencil sharpener or bothering the kid next to me. I just couldn't seem to just... be. So, yeah, this feeling of needing to be in motion, it’s not just the new office setup. It’s been trailing me around since I was in short pants, really. It’s just that now, at 57, you’d think I’d have figured out how to just sit in a chair, right? But it's like the older I get, the more that old grade school kid comes out when I'm forced to just sit there.",
