    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Outside of the physical restlessness, do you struggle to keep your focus on tasks that require sustained mental effort?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S002",
      "patient_response": "Yeah, I do. It’s... well, it’s frustrating, to be honest. I mean, take these coaching meetings we have to do now. They’re these long, drawn-out sessions where the supervisor goes over call metrics and quality assurance and all this new protocol stuff. And I go in there, you know, I sit down, I've got my notebook open, I've got my pen ready, and I tell myself, \"Okay, pay attention, this is important.\" And for the first couple of minutes, I'm right there with them. I'm nodding, I'm tracking what they're saying. \n\nBut then, it's like... I don't even know how it happens. It's like a switch flips in my brain, but without me touching it. Suddenly, I'll realize that the supervisor is still talking, their mouth is moving, but I haven't heard a single word they've said for the last five minutes. My mind is just... gone. I'm thinking about what I'm going to make for dinner, or remembering a movie I saw last week, or wondering why the ceiling tiles have that weird pattern. And it's the same thing when they hand out a new procedure manual or an update on the software we use. I'll start reading, and I'll get through a paragraph, maybe two, and then I'll realize my eyes are just scanning the words. They're moving across the page, line by line, but nothing is actually going in. I'm not absorbing any of it. I have to go back and re-read the same sentence three or four times just to figure out what it's supposed to mean, because my brain just refused to grab onto it the first time. It's incredibly draining, trying to force your brain to just stay on the track when it wants to go everywhere else.",
      "new_confirmed_symptoms": [
        "S002",
