    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "Do you find yourself struggling with constant, uncontrollable worry about a variety of everyday matters?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "No, not really. I mean, I wouldn't call it worry, you know? It's not like I'm sitting around chewing my fingernails about paying the electric bill or whether the car is going to break down or, I don't know, what the neighbors think of my lawn. I mean, sure, I have the normal stuff—everyone thinks about bills and work and all that—but it doesn't feel like a constant, uncontrollable dread or anything like that. It's more just... noise, I guess. \n\nLike, my brain is always busy, always buzzing, but it's not usually buzzing about bad things that might happen. It's buzzing about everything. Like I said before, it's the ceiling tiles, or a song I heard on the radio on the way to work, or wondering if I should try making chili this weekend instead of my usual spaghetti. It's just a constant stream of thoughts, a lot of jumping around, but it doesn't have that anxious, heavy feeling to it. I'm not lying awake at night terrified of the future, I'm lying awake at night because my brain decided it's the perfect time to remember every embarrassing thing I said in 1994, or to try and redesign my kitchen in my head. So, no, the worry isn't really the issue. It's just the sheer volume of thoughts, not so much what the thoughts are actually about, if that makes sense.",
